  geopolitics: "Day 235 of the Hormuz closure produced the clearest statement yet of what this market believes the war is. At 03:18 UTC a Kuwait-flagged tanker carrying 21 Indian seafarers was struck near the strait, UKMTO confirming and all crew reported safe; a projectile hit another tanker at 18:01 UTC yesterday, explosions were reported in Iran's port city of Sirik with Israel's Channel 12 carrying single-source claims that US forces struck an anti-ship missile launcher, and sea mines have resurfaced as a threat after Iran claimed on October 17 that two tankers caught fire striking them. Four transits were logged on October 18 against a pre-crisis baseline of 85 - five percent of normal traffic - and war-risk insurance for a VLCC runs about 32 times pre-crisis levels. Oil did what it should: WTI rose 1.76% to 98.863 and Brent 2.02% to 111.013, with the 12.15 dollar spread between them the market's price for transit risk specifically, since Hormuz bites the seaborne grade. And gold fell 1.17% to 4,092.52, through the 4,100 support, while silver fell 2.75% to 56.361. A tanker is hit and the inflation hedge is sold. That is a market that has fully converted a shipping war into a monetary event: it believes central banks will respond, it is pricing those responses - the RBNZ at nearly 98%, the Bank of Canada at 75-87%, the Bank of England locked for November 5, the Fed near 90% for December - and with the TIPS ten-year real yield at 2.73 the rates channel is now beating the haven channel so decisively that the metals complex has begun to break. Silver at minus 12.54% on the month is more than twice gold's decline, because the industrial leg is going at the same time: copper fell 1.43% to 6.4492 with LME inventories up 8% over the week and ex-China supply fears easing, from a record 14,875 dollars a tonne set on September 10. The FX transmission has changed character again. For three weeks the oil premium was a repricing engine; today it was fully priced, and the four currencies it repriced went nowhere or backwards - the kiwi and the Aussie were the two weakest legs on the board. What is left is the two currencies that could not reprice, and they are not symmetric. Japan's position improved: the Bloomberg survey for a December hike went from 35% a month ago to 83%, national core CPI lands tonight at 23:30 UTC with a 2.1% consensus from 1.7%, and the yen led all seven crosses even though its September trade deficit of 536.6 billion yen against 181.0 billion expected is the purest arithmetic of an energy importer paying 111 dollar Brent. The euro area's did not: EU gas at 87.88 euros per megawatt hour is up 22.31% on the month and German gas 21.17%, the ECB has said 2.50% already sits within neutral, and the French ten-year cleared 5% for the first time since 2002 with the Bund spread at 158.6 basis points - a terms-of-trade tax and a sovereign-credit problem with no policy answer to either. Yet the euro rose for a second session, which is why this report has moved the French expression out of spot and into EU50 at minus 2.22% on the month. The invalidation is unchanged and has become more concrete rather than less. Iran's seven-day plan would reopen the strait on day six and start final-agreement talks on day seven if Washington lifts the port blockade and oil sanctions, ends regional hostilities and releases frozen assets; Foreign Minister Araghchi says Iran is ready for a deal but does not trust Trump, who rejected an earlier version in late September; Qatar and France called for restoring freedom of navigation at 01:45 UTC today and Britain and Oman agreed on the need to open the waterway. The obstacle is nuclear - Washington wants part of the enriched uranium removed and wants it addressed from the outset. A breakthrough there removes the single common input behind four central-bank repricings simultaneously, and because those repricings are now fully paid for, the unwind would be faster and larger than it would have been a week ago.",
